{
  "_about": "Hand adjudication of whether a stored Sidewalk label's pixel sits on, or behind, an object the GSV depth model does not contain. Occlusion cannot be detected geometrically: an unmodelled car or hedge occupies the ray, the ray passes through it, and the payload returns the ground behind -- which is exactly the flat-earth prediction for that depression angle. Only imagery separates the two, so these verdicts were made by eye against the committed adjudication imagery.",
  "_method": "Sample: cleaned labels on panoramas that have adjudication-zoom imagery and a known yaw, shuffled with pandas random_state=7, first 36 taken. Each viewed as a 360 px crop centred on depth_validation.label_pixel_in_image, which applies the heading-centred raster rotation.",
  "_reviewer": "Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5[1m]), 2026-08-06, against data/depth-validation-tiles.jsonl.gz",
  "_correction": "An earlier pass over the same 36 labels reported 6 occlusions. It was wrong: it placed markers with the north-referenced sv_image_x against a heading-centred raster, so the crops were centred up to half a panorama away from the label and hit parked cars at roughly the rate cars appear in the scene. Superseded by this pass; see reports/2026-08-06-depth-coordinate-conventions.md.",
